>take fish and examine it
Taken.
A Babel fish: a small yellow thing with the look of a leech that has found
religion. It lives on the brainwave energy of whoever is nearby and, in return,
makes every language they hear come out as their own. You have always been
faintly suspicious of how good a deal that is.

>drop it then take it
Dropped.
Taken.

>look; inventory

Arthur's Hut
A single round room of mud, thatch and optimism. Everything in it is exactly
where you left it, because there is nowhere else for it to be. A shelf runs
along one wall, and a bed, which is a bed on a generous day, takes up the other.
The doorway opens east onto the village green.

On the wooden shelf are a pencil sketch and a glass jar.
You're carrying:
  a Babel fish
  a dressing gown (being worn)

>e and then e

The Village Green
A trampled circle of grass ringed by a dozen houses like your own. Smoke rises,
unhurried, from one or two chimneys. Your house is to the west, the sandwich hut
stands to the east, and a path runs north to the meadow where the occasional
ship puts down.

The Sandwich Hut
A low hut with a wide shutter that folds down into a counter. This is where your
life happens. Shelves and a cool pit hold the ingredients, a chopping board sits
in the middle of the counter, and the knife hangs from its nail. The village
green is back to the west.

On the counter is a sandwich knife. On the chopping board is a loaf of bread. In
the cool pit is a cured meat. On the pantry shelf are some sourberries and some
pickled roots.

Hob leans on the counter with the air of a man who has been waiting since before
the sun. 

